PG.notes("l123", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the first boss-level project: a two-player dice page that rolls random dice on every refresh and announces the winner. Starter HTML/CSS is supplied so the focus stays on DOM and JavaScript, with an option to rebuild the HTML/CSS from scratch.</p>\n<h3>What the finished page does</h3>\n<ul><li>An h1 headline reads 'Refresh Me' on first load.</li><li>Below it are two dice panels labelled Player 1 and Player 2, each with a dice image.</li><li>Every page refresh rolls two random values, swaps both images to match, and replaces the headline with the result (which player wins, or a draw).</li><li>Purpose of the project: apply the DOM, selection, attribute and text manipulation skills from the previous section in a standalone build.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to approach it</h3>\n<ul><li>Use the provided starter project (next lecture) with HTML and CSS done, so only JS needs writing.</li><li>Optional extra practice: ignore the starter markup, open the finished Dicee page in Chrome from the skeleton zip and recreate the HTML and CSS to match. The zip includes the dice image assets.</li><li>Recommended practice is to concentrate on one new skill at a time, which is why the skeleton is provided.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Dicee Game</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Skeleton project with index.html, styles.css and the dice images (dice1.png to dice6.png), with both players showing dice 6 initially.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> On every refresh, roll two random dice, show the matching images, and announce the winner in the h1.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Download the skeleton and open it.</li><li>Add an external JavaScript file and link it (steps follow in the next articles).</li><li>Generate two random numbers from 1 to 6.</li><li>Update both img elements to the matching dice image.</li><li>Change the h1 text to show Player 1 wins, Player 2 wins, or Draw.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Refreshing the page shows two random dice and a headline naming the winner or a draw.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var randomNumber1 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\nvar randomNumber2 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\n\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.img1&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, &quot;./images/dice&quot; + randomNumber1 + &quot;.png&quot;);\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.img2&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, &quot;./images/dice&quot; + randomNumber2 + &quot;.png&quot;);\n\nif (randomNumber1 === randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Draw!&quot;;\n} else if (randomNumber1 &gt; randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 1 Wins!&quot;;\n} else {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 2 Wins!&quot;;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reference repo adds emoji flags and a personal footer, which are not part of the lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l124", "<p class=\"summary\">Admin article: the Dicee starter files are available from the course's shared resources list page; download and unzip them before starting.</p>\n<h3>Getting the starter files</h3>\n<ul><li>Starter files come from the course's resource list site (appbrewery.co web development resources page), linked in the lecture.</li><li>Download the zip and extract it before opening it in the editor.</li><li>The skeleton contains the Dicee HTML, CSS and dice images (dice1 to dice6).</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Step 0: Get the skeleton</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Nothing yet.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Have the skeleton project unzipped locally and open in the editor.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the resources list page linked in the lecture.</li><li>Download the Dicee skeleton zip.</li><li>Unzip it into your projects folder and open the folder in VS Code.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A folder containing index.html, styles.css and an images folder.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Opening the zip without extracting it first.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l125", "<p class=\"summary\">Step 1 of the Dicee challenge: add a new JavaScript file and link it from the HTML page so later steps have somewhere to put code.</p>\n<h3>External script file</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a JS file in the project folder (the lecture names it index.js; the reference repo calls it script.js, any name works if the link matches).</li><li>Link it from index.html with a script tag whose src points at the file.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Put the script tag just before the closing body tag so the elements it selects already exist when it runs; alternatively use defer in the head.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Linking the script at the end of the body</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;!-- page content --&gt;\n  &lt;script src=&quot;./script.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Step 1: external JS file</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Skeleton Dicee project with HTML and CSS only.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Have a JS file loading on the page.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create a new JavaScript file in the project folder.</li><li>Add a script tag with a matching src in index.html, placed just before &lt;/body&gt;.</li><li>Add a console.log in the file and check the browser console to confirm it runs.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Page loads with no errors and the console message appears.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;script src=&quot;./script.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Wrong file path or name in src, so the script silently does not load.</li><li>Placing the tag in the head without defer, so querySelector returns null later.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l126", "<p class=\"summary\">Step 2: point both img elements at the dice6.png file from the images folder.</p>\n<h3>Setting image sources</h3>\n<ul><li>The project has an images folder with dice1.png to dice6.png.</li><li>Each player's img element needs a src value of the relative path to dice6.png.</li><li>The reference markup gives each img its own class (img1 and img2) so JavaScript can select them separately, and an alt attribute for accessibility.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Relative paths start from the HTML file's location, so ./images/dice6.png is correct when images sits next to index.html.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Player 1 image element</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;div class=&quot;dice&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;Player 1&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;img class=&quot;img1&quot; src=&quot;./images/dice6.png&quot; alt=&quot;Dice showing six&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Step 2: dice images</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Dicee page with img elements.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Both dice show the six face.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Set src on the first img to the dice6.png path.</li><li>Do the same for the second img.</li><li>Open the page and confirm two six-faced dice.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Two dice images appear, one per player.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;img class=&quot;img1&quot; src=&quot;./images/dice6.png&quot; alt=&quot;Dice showing six&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;img class=&quot;img2&quot; src=&quot;./images/dice6.png&quot; alt=&quot;Dice showing six&quot; /&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Broken image icon from a wrong relative path.</li><li>The reference repo has a garbled alt text ('Given Away' split over lines), which should be a proper description.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l127", "<p class=\"summary\">Step 3: in the script, create randomNumber1 holding a random whole number from 1 to 6 and verify it in the console.</p>\n<h3>Random integer from 1 to 6</h3>\n<ul><li>Math.random() returns a decimal from 0 up to but not including 1.</li><li>Multiply by 6 to get 0 up to (not including) 6.</li><li>Math.floor rounds down to a whole number, giving 0-5.</li><li>Add 1 to shift the range to 1-6.</li><li>Store the result in a variable named randomNumber1 and run it repeatedly to check the range.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Dice roll formula</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var randomNumber1 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1; // 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6</pre>\n<h2>The task: Step 3: random number</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Empty script linked to the page.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A variable with a random value 1-6.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Declare randomNumber1.</li><li>Assign Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1.</li><li>Log it and refresh several times to see values vary.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Console shows different integers between 1 and 6.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var randomNumber1 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\nconsole.log(randomNumber1);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting + 1, which produces 0-5.</li><li>Using Math.round, which skews the odds of 1 and 6.</li><li>Writing Math.random() * 6 + 1 without floor, giving decimals.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l128", "<p class=\"summary\">Step 4: use the random number to build an image file name and apply it to the left img with setAttribute.</p>\n<h3>Building the src from the number</h3>\n<ul><li>Dice image names follow a pattern: dice1.png to dice6.png, so the random number can be spliced into the path with string concatenation.</li><li>Select the left image by its class, then call setAttribute('src', newPath).</li><li>Hint in the lecture: use the DOM selection and setAttribute techniques from the previous section.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Update the left die</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;.img1&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, &quot;./images/dice&quot; + randomNumber1 + &quot;.png&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Step 4: random left die</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> randomNumber1 exists; both images show dice6.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Left image changes to the rolled face on refresh.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Build the string './images/dice' + randomNumber1 + '.png'.</li><li>Select the left img with querySelector.</li><li>Call setAttribute('src', thatString).</li><li>Refresh a few times.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Left die shows a different face on refreshes, right die still six.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">document.querySelector(&quot;.img1&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, &quot;./images/dice&quot; + randomNumber1 + &quot;.png&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Missing the leading ./ or the .png extension.</li><li>Using .img1 without the dot in querySelector.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l129", "<p class=\"summary\">Step 5: repeat the previous step for the right-hand die using a second random number.</p>\n<h3>Second player</h3>\n<ul><li>Create randomNumber2 with the same formula.</li><li>Select the second img (class img2) and set its src with randomNumber2.</li><li>The two rolls are independent, so equal values are possible and need handling in the next step.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Both dice</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var randomNumber2 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.img2&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, &quot;./images/dice&quot; + randomNumber2 + &quot;.png&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Step 5: both dice</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Left die already randomised.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Both dice show independent random faces.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Declare randomNumber2.</li><li>Set the img2 src from it.</li><li>Refresh and confirm the dice differ at times.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each refresh shows two independent faces.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var randomNumber2 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.img2&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, &quot;./images/dice&quot; + randomNumber2 + &quot;.png&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reusing randomNumber1 for both images, so dice always match.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l130", "<p class=\"summary\">Step 6: compare the two numbers and set the h1 to announce Player 1, Player 2 or a draw.</p>\n<h3>Winner logic</h3>\n<ul><li>Compare randomNumber1 and randomNumber2 with an if / else if / else chain: equal means draw, higher left means Player 1, otherwise Player 2.</li><li>Use strict equality (===) for the draw check.</li><li>Select the h1 and set its innerHTML (or textContent) to the result string.</li><li>Lecture hint: if statement, querySelector and innerHTML are all that is needed.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Winner announcement</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">if (randomNumber1 === randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Draw!&quot;;\n} else if (randomNumber1 &gt; randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 1 Wins!&quot;;\n} else {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 2 Wins!&quot;;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Step 6: show the winner</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Both dice randomised; heading says Refresh Me.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Heading reports which player won or a draw.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Write an if comparing the two values for equality.</li><li>Add else if for Player 1 higher.</li><li>Add else for Player 2.</li><li>In each branch change the h1 content.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Heading text always matches the dice faces shown.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">if (randomNumber1 === randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Draw!&quot;;\n} else if (randomNumber1 &gt; randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 1 Wins!&quot;;\n} else {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 2 Wins!&quot;;\n}\n// reference repo adds flag emoji to the messages; optional</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using = instead of === in the condition.</li><li>Wrong comparison direction mislabeling the winner.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l131", "<p class=\"summary\">Step-by-step walkthrough of the Dicee solution: link an external script, set default dice images, generate two random 1-6 numbers, build image paths with string concatenation, update both img elements with setAttribute, and announce the winner in the h1 with an if / else if / else chain. Ends with advice on balancing short and readable code.</p>\n<h3>Step 1: external script</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a JS file (index.js) in the project and link it with a script tag whose src is that file.</li><li>Place the tag just before the closing body tag, the same position used for other external resources in earlier modules.</li><li>Quick test: put an alert in the file, reload, and see the alert to confirm the link works. Remove it afterwards.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: default images</h3>\n<ul><li>Both img src values start as images/dice6.png. Dice files live in an images folder and are named dice1 to dice6, so the folder must be part of the path.</li><li>The src must include the folder name (plural 'images') followed by a slash.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: random number recap</h3>\n<ul><li>Math.random() gives a decimal from 0 up to just under 1.</li><li>Multiplying by 6 widens it to 0 up to just under 6.</li><li>Math.floor drops the decimals, leaving integers 0-5.</li><li>Adding 1 shifts the range to 1-6.</li><li>Any piece of code can be pasted into the console to inspect its output, which helps verify each step.</li><li>If this is unclear, revisit the earlier randomisation lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 4: building the image path</h3>\n<ul><li>Because file names are dice1.png to dice6.png, string concatenation of 'dice' + number + '.png' picks the right file.</li><li>A second variable prepends 'images/'. This split into separate variables is optional and used only to make each step visible; one expression would do.</li><li>Select the first image with document.querySelectorAll('img')[0]. querySelectorAll returns a list of all matching elements, so index 0 is the first (left) image and index 1 is the second.</li><li>Call setAttribute('src', randomImageSource) on it: first argument is the attribute name, second is the new value.</li><li>After reloading, the left die shows a random face each time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 5: second image</h3>\n<ul><li>Repeat with a second variable randomNumber2 using the same formula, a second source string, and querySelectorAll('img')[1].</li><li>Recommendation: type the repeated code out instead of copy-pasting, because pasted duplicates tend to leave stale variable names that cause bugs.</li><li>The second version condenses lines to show that the variable and select/set steps can be merged.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 6: announcing the winner</h3>\n<ul><li>Use an if / else if / else: first branch randomNumber1 greater than randomNumber2 means Player 1 wins; else if randomNumber2 is greater means Player 2 wins; the final else covers equal values, a draw.</li><li>Each branch sets innerHTML of document.querySelector('h1'). querySelector is enough because the page has one h1.</li><li>Emoji can be added to the message text: on Mac via Edit &gt; Emoji &amp; Symbols, on Windows by searching for the emoji in the browser.</li></ul>\n<h3>Alternatives and code readability</h3>\n<ul><li>Other valid approaches: getElementById, getElementsByTagName/ClassName, or querySelector with classes (the starter gives the images classes) instead of querySelectorAll indexes.</li><li>The code can be refactored shorter, but compress with care. Very short code that crams many operations into one line is hard to read.</li><li>Aim for a balance between concise and expressive. The walkthrough version is deliberately somewhat long to be clear.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Prefer let/const over var in modern code, and a function for the repeated roll logic.</li><li>Next module covers responding to events such as clicks and key presses.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Complete solution (from the lecture, cross-checked with the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// index.js, linked just before &lt;/body&gt;\nvar randomNumber1 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\nvar randomDiceImage = &quot;dice&quot; + randomNumber1 + &quot;.png&quot;;\nvar randomImageSource = &quot;images/&quot; + randomDiceImage;\nvar image1 = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;img&quot;)[0];\nimage1.setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, randomImageSource);\n\nvar randomNumber2 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\nvar randomImageSource2 = &quot;images/dice&quot; + randomNumber2 + &quot;.png&quot;;\ndocument.querySelectorAll(&quot;img&quot;)[1].setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, randomImageSource2);\n\nif (randomNumber1 &gt; randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 1 Wins!&quot;;\n} else if (randomNumber2 &gt; randomNumber1) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 2 Wins!&quot;;\n} else {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Draw!&quot;;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Compact variant from the reference repo with class selectors and a draw check first</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var randomNumber1 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\nvar randomNumber2 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\n\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.img1&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, &quot;./images/dice&quot; + randomNumber1 + &quot;.png&quot;);\ndocument.querySelector(&quot;.img2&quot;).setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, &quot;./images/dice&quot; + randomNumber2 + &quot;.png&quot;);\n\nif (randomNumber1 === randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Draw!&quot;;\n} else if (randomNumber1 &gt; randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 1 Wins!&quot;;\n} else {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 2 Wins!&quot;;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Dicee game</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Skeleton project: index.html with h1 'Refresh Me', two img elements, styles.css, images folder (dice1-6).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> On each refresh show two random dice faces and a headline naming the winner or a draw.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create index.js and link it before &lt;/body&gt;; test with an alert.</li><li>Set both img src values to images/dice6.png.</li><li>Create randomNumber1 with Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1.</li><li>Build the image path with concatenation and set the first img src via setAttribute.</li><li>Repeat for randomNumber2 and the second img.</li><li>Compare the numbers with if / else if / else and set the h1 innerHTML.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Refresh gives two random dice and a matching result message.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// index.js, linked just before &lt;/body&gt;\nvar randomNumber1 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\nvar randomDiceImage = &quot;dice&quot; + randomNumber1 + &quot;.png&quot;;\nvar randomImageSource = &quot;images/&quot; + randomDiceImage;\nvar image1 = document.querySelectorAll(&quot;img&quot;)[0];\nimage1.setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, randomImageSource);\n\nvar randomNumber2 = Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;\nvar randomImageSource2 = &quot;images/dice&quot; + randomNumber2 + &quot;.png&quot;;\ndocument.querySelectorAll(&quot;img&quot;)[1].setAttribute(&quot;src&quot;, randomImageSource2);\n\nif (randomNumber1 &gt; randomNumber2) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 1 Wins!&quot;;\n} else if (randomNumber2 &gt; randomNumber1) {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Player 2 Wins!&quot;;\n} else {\n  document.querySelector(&quot;h1&quot;).innerHTML = &quot;Draw!&quot;;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting + 1 so dice 0 is requested (broken image).</li><li>Using querySelector (single) for the images and getting only the first one, or off-by-one indexes with querySelectorAll.</li><li>Copy-pasting code and leaving randomNumber1 in the second block.</li><li>Missing 'images/' folder prefix in the src.</li><li>Reference repo differs slightly: it uses class selectors .img1 / .img2, ./images/ paths, === first for draw, and adds flag emoji and a personal footer (ignore the footer).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l132", "<p class=\"summary\">Admin: the finished Dicee code is downloadable from the lecture resources if you are stuck or want to compare.</p>\n<h3>Completed code</h3>\n<ul><li>Resources attached to the lecture hold the full finished project.</li><li>Use it to check your solution or when stuck, after trying the challenge yourself.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l133", "<p class=\"summary\">A focus tip: schedule the hardest study or work early in the day, before eating, since concentration tends to dip after a heavy meal.</p>\n<h3>Study on an empty stomach</h3>\n<ul><li>Do the most demanding or most postponed task first thing in the morning, before a meal.</li><li>Heavy, carb-rich lunches tend to make people drowsy and less able to concentrate.</li><li>A lighter lunch can help sustain clearer thinking later in the day.</li><li>Personal suggestion only: skip it if you have a medical condition such as diabetes or anything else that makes fasting unsafe.</li></ul>");
